---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Capture from '../components/Capture.astro'
import SurfaceLab from '../components/SurfaceLab.astro'
import EngineDrawing from '../components/EngineDrawing.astro'
import {
  bundled,
  connectors,
  doc,
  featureIndex,
  providers,
  releases,
  repo,
  shot,
  subscriptions,
} from '../lib/content'
---

<Page>
  <section class="hero container" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">
        <span class="tiny-cross" aria-hidden="true">+</span> A desktop surface for the pi coding agent
      </p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Same agent.<br />Your <span>surface.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">
        Your models. Your extensions. Your way of working.<br class="desktop-break" /> Phosphor puts the
        agent, the code and the result in one desktop workspace.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="action action-primary" href="#install"
          >Install Phosphor <span aria-hidden="true">↘</span></a
        ><a class="action action-text" href="#surface"
          >Explore the surface <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="hero-meta mono">
        <span>macOS / Linux / Windows</span><span>Open source · MIT</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <EngineDrawing />
    <div class="hero-baseline">
      <span class="mono">BUILT BY ENGINEERS / FOR ENGINEERS</span><p>
        Not a new agent loop. A place to work with the one you chose.
      </p><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <SurfaceLab />

  <section id="models" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">02 / Models & accounts</p><h2>
            Use the plan<br />you already pay for.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Sign in instead of reaching for an API key. Phosphor exposes pi’s account logins and
          installed providers in the same workspace.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="subscription-grid">
        {
          subscriptions.map(({ mark, name, plan, route }) => (
            <article class="subscription">
              <>
                <div class="subscription-top">
                  <>
                    <span class="provider-initial" aria-hidden="true">
                      {mark}
                    </span>
                    <span class="mono">SUBSCRIPTION</span>
                  </>
                </div>
                <h3>{name}</h3>
                <p class="plan">{plan}</p>
                <p>{route}</p>
              </>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Your provider’s plan limits and billing rules still apply. Claude subscription sessions use <code
          >@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code
        >, not native Anthropic OAuth. <a class="text-link" href={doc('settings.md#accounts')}
          >Sign-in routes ↗</a
        >
      </p>
      <div class="model-detail split">
        <div class="copy-column">
          <p class="eyebrow">Not tied to one provider</p><h3 class="subheading">
            Pick the model<br />for the next turn.
          </h3><p>
            Search the models you have access to. Star the ones you use. Switch mid-session and
            carry the conversation with you. Thinking levels appear when the model supports them.
          </p><p>
            API keys and custom or local endpoints work too. What is available depends on your pi
            install, credentials and provider packages.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('chat.md#session-header--status-strip-per-session')}
            >Model controls ↗</a
          >
        </div>
        <Capture
          image={shot('models')}
          alt="Phosphor’s searchable model menu over a real conversation, grouped by provider."
          caption="The model menu, in the composer"
        />
      </div>
      <div class="provider-index">
        <span class="mono">PI SPEAKS TO</span><ul>
          {providers.map((provider) => <li>{provider}</li>)}
        </ul><a class="text-link" href={`${repo}#phosphor`}>Provider overview ↗</a>
      </div>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Thinking levels and sign-in, in the app <span>+</span></summary><div
          class="capture-pair"
        >
          <Capture
            image={shot('thinking')}
            alt="The thinking-level menu above the composer in a real Phosphor session."
            caption="Effort is a model control"
          /><Capture
            image={shot('accounts')}
            alt="The Accounts settings screen with subscription providers and sign-in status; account email is redacted."
            caption="Provider sign-in status"
          />
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="gateways" class="section gateway-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / The gateways</p><h2>
            Know what’s connected.<br />Know what it costs.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          An account can run out. A tool schema takes space. A long conversation fills the window.
          Those should be visible facts, not surprises.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="gateway-grid">
        <article class="gateway">
          <div class="gateway-heading">
            <span class="mono">A / ACCOUNT</span><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
          </div><h3>Which account<br />is this lane using?</h3><p>
            Keep several Claude logins. Pick one, route in order or round-robin new sessions. Open
            an account to see its usage windows and the sessions spending it.
          </p><div class="account-route" aria-label="Diagram of Claude account routing">
            <span>New session</span><i>↓</i><strong>Account routing</strong><div>
              <span>Specific</span><span>Ordered</span><span>Round-robin</span>
            </div>
          </div><p class="gateway-caveat">
            Running sessions keep their account. Moving one to another account restarts it from its
            saved session; the next turn re-reads the thread.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('settings.md#accounts')}>Account gateway ↗</a>
        </article>
        <article class="gateway">
          <div class="gateway-heading">
            <span class="mono">M / MCP</span><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
          </div><h3>Connect tools.<br />Load detail on demand.</h3><p>
            The MCP gateway exposes a proxy per server. The agent discovers tool details when it
            needs them, rather than carrying every schema in every request.
          </p><div class="mcp-route" aria-label="Diagram of MCP discovery">
            <span>pi</span><b>→</b><span>MCP gateway</span><b>→</b><span>Server tools</span>
          </div><p class="gateway-caveat">
            Direct tools are an explicit exception. MCP OAuth and credential storage belong to the
            adapter, not Phosphor.
          </p><a
            class="text-link"
            href={doc('mcp.md#the-claude-provider-reaches-mcp-through-pi-not-around-it')}
            >MCP gateway ↗</a
          >
        </article>
        <article class="gateway">
          <div class="gateway-heading">
            <span class="mono">C / CONTEXT</span><span aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
          </div><h3>Full of what,<br />exactly?</h3><p>
            Open the live meter for messages, system prompt, tools and MCP schemas, alongside token
            and cache counts and reported cost.
          </p><div class="context-key">
            <span>pi’s total <b>Authoritative</b></span><span>Component sizes <b>Estimated</b></span
            ><span>Provider overhead <b>Unmeasured</b></span>
          </div><p class="gateway-caveat">
            Claude sessions add their own account’s usage windows and reported plan limits. Missing
            telemetry is not shown as zero.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('chat.md#what-the-context-meters-popover-shows')}
            >Read the context meter ↗</a
          >
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="gateway-captures split">
        <Capture
          image={shot('context')}
          alt="A real context popover showing estimated composition, MCP server attribution, tokens, session cost and Claude plan usage."
          caption="Context, with the source of each number"
        /><div class="copy-column">
          <p class="eyebrow">Tools from your actual stack</p><h3 class="subheading">
            Your services.<br />In the same session.
          </h3><p>
            Mount a curated connector or add a custom URL or command-based MCP server. Inspect its
            configuration, sign in, reconnect and test it without a model call.
          </p><ul class="connector-list">{connectors.map((name) => <li>{name}</li>)}</ul><p
            class="fineprint"
          >
            Some services need extra setup. Slack, for example, requires your own registered app and
            appropriate scopes.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('mcp.md')}>Connector setup and permissions ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Open the connector settings capture <span>+</span></summary><Capture
          image={shot('connectors')}
          alt="Phosphor’s real Connectors settings screen with available MCP services."
          caption="Connectors are configuration, not a second agent"
        />
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="work" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">04 / Lanes, not loose chats</p><h2>
            Several tasks.<br />Separate branches.<br /><span class="muted-heading"
              >One place to look.</span
            >
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          A lane keeps a session, its worktree, branch and PR status together. Each live session
          runs its own pi process. None is pretending to manage the others.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div
        class="lane-flow"
        aria-label="Lane identity: session, branch, worktree, pull request status"
      >
        <span><i>01</i>Session</span><b>→</b><span><i>02</i>Branch</span><b>→</b><span
          ><i>03</i>Worktree</span
        ><b>→</b><span><i>04</i>PR status</span>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('home')}
        alt="Phosphor home, showing the project’s real lanes and the new-session composer."
        caption="Home is where you see what needs you"
      />
      <div class="numbered-notes">
        <article>
          <span>01</span><h3>Start in a separate checkout.</h3><p>
            New tasks can get a worktree and branch by default. Pick a branch prefix, let the first
            task name the lane, or work on the main checkout deliberately.
          </p>
        </article><article>
          <span>02</span><h3>See the next action.</h3><p>
            The home board groups lanes by what they need: waiting on you, ready to merge, needs a
            push, in review or running. The ledger adds spend, tokens and live processes.
          </p>
        </article><article>
          <span>03</span><h3>Keep Git state in sight.</h3><p>
            Search by title, branch or PR. Inspect checks and review state. Update from trunk and
            use the guided local merge flow when the work is ready.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        The board derives from existing state; no model runs to organize it. PR badges use <code
          >gh</code
        > read-only. The merge flow is local, not a GitHub PR merge. <a
          class="text-link"
          href={doc('lanes.md')}>Lane behavior ↗</a
        >
        <a class="text-link" href={doc('worktrees.md')}>Worktree safeguards ↗</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="transcript" class="section transcript-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">05 / The transcript</p><h2>
            Read the work.<br />Not just the answer.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Text streams into the conversation. Thinking has its own block. Tool calls become steps
          you can open. An edit leads to a diff, not a reassuring sentence.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="split transcript-split">
        <Capture
          image={shot('changes')}
          alt="A real agent turn with the activity expanded to show an edit diff and the Changes pane alongside."
          caption="The edit, its diff and the files it changed"
        /><div class="copy-column">
          <h3 class="subheading">A transcript you<br />can inspect.</h3><ul class="feature-lines">
            <li>
              <strong>Open the activity.</strong> Inspect tool arguments and output. Claude-native tool
              markers identify calls but do not expose their result bodies.
            </li><li>
              <strong>Review what changed.</strong> Per-file diffs compare against the session baseline.
              Revert a file from Changes.
            </li><li>
              <strong>Read rich output.</strong> Tables, syntax-highlighted code, Mermaid, charts and
              KaTeX render as content.
            </li><li>
              <strong>Keep your place.</strong> Read back while the agent works; jump to the tail when
              you are ready.
            </li>
          </ul><a class="text-link" href={doc('chat.md')}>Transcript behavior ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="composer-reference">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">A small input. Several ways in.</p><h3>
            Keep working while it works.
          </h3><p>
            Enter steers a running turn. A follow-up waits until the agent finishes. The two queues
            stay distinct, and drafts survive session switches and restarts.
          </p>
        </div><dl>
          <div><dt><code>@</code></dt><dd>Mention a workspace file</dd></div><div>
            <dt><code>/</code></dt><dd>Commands, prompts and skills</dd>
          </div><div><dt><code>!</code></dt><dd>Run a shell line in the conversation</dd></div><div>
            <dt><code>!!</code></dt><dd>Run it without adding it to model context</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>File mentions and commands, in the composer <span>+</span></summary><div
          class="capture-pair"
        >
          <Capture
            image={shot('mentions')}
            alt="The @ file mention menu, resolved against the real workspace."
            caption="Reference the file, not a pasted guess"
          /><Capture
            image={shot('commands')}
            alt="The slash-command menu listing commands in a real Phosphor session."
            caption="pi commands and extension commands"
          />
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">06 / The rest of the desk</p><h2>Use your hands, too.</h2></div><p
          class="section-intro"
        >
          The agent is not the only one who can edit a file or run a command. The tools next to the
          conversation are real tools.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="workbench-grid">
        <article>
          <div class="tool-title">
            <span class="mono">FILES / MONACO</span><span aria-hidden="true">⌘ P</span>
          </div><Capture
            image={shot('files')}
            alt="Workspace explorer and Monaco editor docked to the right of the transcript."
            caption="Explorer + editor tabs"
          /><h3>Open. Read. Edit. Save.</h3><p>
            Create, rename, move, copy and import files or folders. Use multi-selection and
            drag-and-drop. Monaco gives you tabs, syntax highlighting, dirty indicators and a
            conflict bar when an external change meets unsaved work.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('files.md')}>Files and editor details ↗</a>
        </article><article>
          <div class="tool-title">
            <span class="mono">TERMINAL / PTY</span><span aria-hidden="true">⌘ `</span>
          </div><Capture
            image={shot('terminal')}
            alt="A real terminal tab open beside the Phosphor conversation in the workspace."
            caption="Your shell, next to the session"
          /><h3>Run the command yourself.</h3><p>
            Real shell tabs with search, clipboard and scrollback. The terminal is separate from the
            agent’s bash activity. “Run in terminal” pastes a command; you decide when to execute
            it.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('terminal.md')}>Terminal behavior ↗</a>
        </article>
      </div>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Give a pane the whole session region <span>+</span></summary><Capture
          image={shot('files-full')}
          alt="Monaco and the file explorer fullscreened in the main session area, with the sidebar still visible."
          caption="Fullscreen does not discard your split layout"
        />
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="artifacts" class="section artifact-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">07 / Artifacts</p><h2>The result deserves<br />its own surface.</h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          A plan, a diagram or an interactive page should not disappear into chat history. Artifacts
          give substantial deliverables a versioned side panel.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Capture
        image={shot('artifacts')}
        alt="A real long-form document open in the artifact pane beside the session that created it."
        caption="A deliverable you can open, read and revise"
      />
      <div class="artifact-bottom">
        <div class="version-flow" aria-label="Artifact version flow">
          <span>v1 <small>Create</small></span><b>→</b><span>v2 <small>Edit</small></span><b>↔</b
          ><span>Diff <small>Compare</small></span>
        </div><p>
          Preview HTML, Markdown, SVG, Mermaid, code and chart documents. Compare revisions. Reopen
          the session to recover them. The global Artifacts page indexes artifacts from open
          sessions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="fineprint">
        Model-authored HTML runs in an opaque-origin sandbox with no network access. This protects
        the app from the document; it does not sandbox agent tools. <a
          class="text-link"
          href={doc('extensions.md#the-artifact-tools-and-what-each-one-costs')}
          >Artifact tools and versioning ↗</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="extensions" class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">08 / Built on pi, extended by you</p><h2>
            The surface is flexible.<br />So is what runs in it.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          Install pi packages from npm, Git or a local path. Keep them global or project-specific.
          Tools, skills, prompts and themes come through the same extension system.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="extension-layout">
        <div class="copy-column">
          <div class="extension-statement">
            <span class="mono">NOT A FORK OF THE ENGINE</span><code
              >Phosphor<br /><span>↕ typed IPC</span><br />Electron main<br /><span
                >↕ JSONL / stdio</span
              ><br />pi + extensions</code
            >
          </div><p>
            Phosphor talks to pi over RPC; it does not import pi’s implementation. Bundled
            extensions run inside each session’s pi process.
          </p><h3>Claude Code can run under pi.</h3><p>
            The provider extension drives the official Claude Code CLI and brings its stream back
            into pi’s session. pi supplies project instructions, skills and extension tools; Claude
            keeps its native tools, authenticated subscription and persistent process. You can use
            Claude Code’s capabilities without giving up the rest of your pi setup.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('cli-providers.md#current-claude-integration')}
            >The Claude provider boundary ↗</a
          ><p>
            Browse and manage skills from their own page. Bring in optional web, MCP or subagent
            packages when your workflow needs them.
          </p><a class="text-link" href={doc('extensions.md')}>Read the extension contract ↗</a>
        </div><div class="extension-ledger">
          <p class="eyebrow">Six extensions ship with Phosphor</p>{
            bundled.map(([name, description], index) => (
              <article>
                <>
                  <span class="mono">0{index + 1}</span>
                  <div>
                    <>
                      <h3>
                        <code>{name}</code>
                      </h3>
                      <p>{description}</p>
                    </>
                  </div>
                  <span aria-hidden="true">↳</span>
                </>
              </article>
            ))
          }
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="boundary-note">
        <span class="mono">TRUST BOUNDARY</span><p>
          Agent tools run with your machine’s permissions. Installed extensions can execute code.
          Use packages and workspaces you trust. Cloud providers and connected services still
          receive the requests you send them.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="sessions" class="section session-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">09 / Pick up where you left off</p><h2>
            Real sessions.<br />Room for your habits.
          </h2>
        </div><p class="section-intro">
          The session is a pi session, not a private format invented for the UI. Phosphor adds the
          controls to navigate it and a layout that remembers how you work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="session-features">
        <article>
          <span class="mono">HISTORY</span><h3>Take another branch.</h3><p>
            Inspect the session tree, jump to a point, bookmark it, fork or clone. Export the
            conversation as HTML.
          </p>
        </article><article>
          <span class="mono">CONTINUITY</span><h3>Leave. Come back.</h3><p>
            Resume saved sessions. Keep independent pane positions and drafts. Background sessions
            continue streaming while you switch.
          </p>
        </article><article>
          <span class="mono">CONTROL</span><h3>Manage the long run.</h3><p>
            Steer, queue follow-ups, stop, compact or configure auto-retry. Session files are
            written when a turn ends, not on every token.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <fieldset class="theme-study">
        <legend class="sr-only">Choose an appearance capture</legend><input
          class="sr-only"
          type="radio"
          name="appearance"
          id="theme-dark"
          checked
        /><input class="sr-only" type="radio" name="appearance" id="theme-light" /><div
          class="theme-study-header"
        >
          <div>
            <h3>Dark. Light. Your eyes.</h3><p>
              Light, dark or system. UI scale, fonts and chat, editor and terminal sizes are yours
              to set.
            </p>
          </div><div class="theme-controls">
            <label for="theme-dark">Dark</label><label for="theme-light">Light</label>
          </div>
        </div><div class="theme-dark-capture">
          <Capture
            image={shot('artifacts')}
            alt="Phosphor’s dark graphite theme on a real artifact session."
            caption="Graphite and amber"
          />
        </div><div class="theme-light-capture">
          <Capture
            image={shot('light')}
            alt="The same Phosphor artifact session in the light theme with cool paper surfaces."
            caption="Cool paper and ember"
          />
        </div>
      </fieldset>
      <div class="source-line">
        <a class="text-link" href={doc('ui-shell.md')}>Sessions and pane persistence ↗</a><a
          class="text-link"
          href={doc('settings.md#appearance')}>Appearance settings ↗</a
        >
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="install" class="section install-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="install-grid">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">10 / Run it on your machine</p><h2>
            Open a folder.<br />Give it a task.
          </h2><p class="install-lede">
            Install pi, then Phosphor. Sign in to a provider in Settings, pick a model and start a
            session.
          </p><a class="action action-primary" href={`${releases}/latest`}
            >Download a release <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
          ><p class="fineprint">MIT licensed. Provider subscriptions and API usage are separate.</p>
        </div><div class="install-commands">
          <div class="command-block">
            <div><span class="mono">01 / THE ENGINE</span><span>Node ≥ 22.19</span></div><pre
              tabindex="0"
              aria-label="Install the pi coding agent"><code>npm install -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent</code></pre>
          </div><div class="command-block">
            <div><span class="mono">02 / THE DESKTOP — macOS & Linux</span></div><pre
              tabindex="0"
              aria-label="Install Phosphor for macOS or Linux"><code>curl -fsSL https://github.com/agustinsacco/Phosphor/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
          </div><p>
            The installer checks the download against the release checksums. Prefer to inspect it
            first? <a class="text-link" href={`${repo}/blob/main/scripts/install.sh`}
              >Read install.sh ↗</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="platforms">
        <article>
          <h3>macOS</h3><p>DMG or ZIP. Apple silicon and Intel.</p><a
            class="text-link"
            href={`${releases}/latest`}>Release assets ↗</a
          >
        </article><article>
          <h3>Linux</h3><p>AppImage or .deb. The script installs the AppImage.</p><a
            class="text-link"
            href={`${releases}/latest`}>Release assets ↗</a
          >
        </article><article>
          <h3>Windows</h3><p>Installers ship on tagged releases, not every per-merge release.</p><a
            class="text-link"
            href={releases}>Find a tagged release ↗</a
          >
        </article>
      </div>
      <details class="disclosure">
        <summary>Setup, updates and a few useful limits <span>+</span></summary><div
          class="install-details"
        >
          <p>
            <strong>pi must be on PATH.</strong> Until it is available, Phosphor shows a setup screen.
            You can also run <code>pi</code> in the built-in terminal and use <code>/login</code>,
            or configure keys and endpoints in <code>~/.pi/agent/</code>.
          </p><p>
            <strong>Claude is a separate provider package.</strong> Subscription sessions require the
            authenticated Claude Code CLI and <code>@saccolabs/pi-claude-cli</code> ≥ 0.7.1. <a
              class="text-link"
              href={doc('cli-providers.md')}>Provider setup ↗</a
            >
          </p><p>
            <strong>Updates depend on the install.</strong> Packaged apps check at launch and every 30
            minutes. AppImage and eligible macOS installs offer restart-to-update; package-managed or
            read-only installs link to releases. Finish the current turn before restarting. <a
              class="text-link"
              href={doc('updates.md')}>Update paths ↗</a
            >
          </p><p>
            <strong>A coding workspace, not a cloud service.</strong> No cloud sync or orchestration agent.
            Monaco is an editor, not a promise of external language servers or a debugger. <a
              class="text-link"
              href={doc('overview.md')}>Product scope ↗</a
            >
          </p>
        </div>
